Pentium M hybrid branch direction predictor
==========================================

This assignment implements pm_predictor in src/my_predictor.h using the
supplied CBP2 infrastructure by Daniel A. Jimenez. The supplied infrastructure
documentation is in doc/index.html.

Build and run
-------------
Requires a C++11 compiler, make, Python 3, and bzip2/gzip on PATH. No third-party
Python packages or external cache implementation are needed.

Run from this directory:

    make -C src
    ./run traces

For one trace, optionally print exact conditional-branch and direction-miss
counts before the final MPKI result:

    ./src/predict traces/164.gzip/gzip.trace.bz2 --stats

The default make target builds predict. All 20 supplied traces represent
100 million instructions each. MPKI = direction misses / 100000; only
conditional branch direction misses are counted. Lower MPKI is better.

Predictor design
----------------
- Bimodal table: 4096 two-bit saturating counters, indexed by PC[11:0].
- Global table: 512 sets, two ways, six-bit tags, two-bit counters, valid bits,
  and one least-recently-used victim bit per set.
- History: 15 actual conditional outcomes; the newest outcome is the low bit.
- Hash: (PC XOR history) & 0x7fff. Hash[14:6] selects a set; hash[5:0] is its tag.
  PC is the byte address provided by the trace; low address bits are preserved.
- Prediction: use the global counter on a valid tag hit, otherwise the bimodal
  counter. Values 0/1 predict not taken and 2/3 predict taken.
- Initialization: counters start at 1 (weakly not taken), history is zero,
  and all global entries are invalid.
- Update: train the bimodal counter on every conditional outcome. On a global
  miss allocate an invalid way first, otherwise the LRU way. Following the
  assignment's Appendix B example, train the selected slot's existing counter
  instead of resetting it when its tag changes. Refresh LRU on hits and fills.
  Use the saved prediction-time indexes, then shift the actual outcome into
  the masked history register. Unconditional branches do not train this state.

This implements the permitted two-way direction-prediction design. Loop
prediction, branch-target prediction, and the PIR-specific hash are outside
the required scope. The simulator updates after each trace branch and does
not model speculative pipeline timing.

Results and report
------------------
results/summary.csv contains the exact conditional counts, direction misses,
and unrounded MPKI for every supplied benchmark. results/results.txt contains
the corresponding simulator output and mean MPKI.

The report is ../Branch_Prediction_Assignment_Report.docx.

Focused correctness checks
--------------------------
The checks exercise the counter states, hit/fallback selection, hash, saved
prediction context, invalid-way allocation, LRU replacement, and history.

    c++ -std=c++11 tests/test_pm_predictor.cc -o tests/test_pm_predictor
    ./tests/test_pm_predictor
